      "answer": "$x=\\frac14$ is a maximum and $x=\\frac34$ is a minimum.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "1. $\\frac{dy}{dx}=[8]+[-2][(2x-1)^{-2}]$ — B2,1,0.\n2. $=0\\Rightarrow4(2x-1)^2=1$, e.g. $16x^2-16x+3=0$ — M1 — set to zero, simplify and attempt to solve.\n3. $x=\\frac14$ and $\\frac34$ — A1 — needs both $x$ values; ignore $y$ values.\n4. $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}=8(2x-1)^{-3}$ — B1ft — FT to $k(2x-1)^{-3}$ where $k>0$.\n5. At $x=\\frac14$, $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}=-64$ and/or $<0$: maximum — DB1.\n6. At $x=\\frac34$, $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}=64$ and/or $>0$: minimum — DB1.\nGuidance: alternative methods for the last 3 marks must indicate which $x$ values; cannot use $x=\\frac12$.",
